<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Internal Security Act 1960</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1960–2012</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Internal Security Act 1960 was a Malaysian law allowing detention without trial, introduced at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a> to fight communist insurgency. It let the government hold people for renewable two-year periods and was used against politicians, activists and other opponents, notably in Operation Lalang of 1987. It was repealed in 2012 and replaced by the Security Offences (Special Measures) Act.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/internal-security-act-1960.jpg" alt="Internal Security Act 1960" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file: · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Federation_of_Malaya.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
